    {
      "path": "01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_2/Externalities_and_Welfare_Optimum.md",
      "sha256": "529d959710600922cf0919bc4bb77ccd698aa1e4c411e91dcc19de2023f47e39",
      "bytes": 28565,
      "note_sections": [
        "Externalities and Welfare Optimum",
        "Topic map of the Verhoef chapter",
        "What welfare criteria can establish",
        "Identify the externality before proposing a remedy",
        "Types and boundaries",
        "Derive the private and social activity levels",
        "Why a cost reduction is not a new external benefit",
        "Four different policy concepts",
        "Coasean bargaining and its limitations",
        "Efficiency and equity lead to different comparisons",
        "The next-chapter fragment included in the file",
        "What to retain",
        "Sources and page convention"
      ],
      "objective_ids": [
        "O005",
        "O006",
        "O007"
      ],
      "semantic_baseline": "A neglected real receptor welfare variable distinguishes technological externality from physical-only, pecuniary and deliberate relations. Strict Pareto differs from Kaldor winners-could-compensate and Hicks initial-losers-cannot-prevent-by-compensation. Smooth private MPB=MPC versus negative social MPB=MPC+MEC or positive MPB+MEB=MPC; concavity and welfare integrals permit remaining optimal damage. Compensation, internalisation, regulation and optimisation differ. Coase requires enforceable rights, feasible transfers and low transaction costs; efficiency does not fix distribution. Figure13.2 A–E derives private/social/tax/bargaining bounds; D+E upper requires truthful producer/no above-Q0 threat. Marginal incentive differs from total damage and income-sensitive equity. Next-chapter risk text is an opening fragment only.",
      "change_class": "new_content",
      "previous_path": null,
      "previous_hash": null,
      "affected_objective_ids": [
        "O005",
        "O006",
        "O007"
      ],
      "inspection_status": "read_finished_note"
    },
    {
      "path": "01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_2/Market_Based_Environmental_Instruments.md",
      "sha256": "9e2f739fb2dec5a81d11a4afc2b62308b70ad5ab27a0310d07f641df4eb7d87a",
      "bytes": 16348,
      "note_sections": [
        "Market Based Environmental Instruments",
        "Four instrument families",
        "Tradable rights and flexibility",
        "Flexibility across time",
        "Flexibility across space",
        "Why common marginal costs minimize total abatement costs",
        "Innovation and environmental effectiveness",
        "Historical policy examples and chart interpretation",
        "Different ETS metrics answer different questions",
        "Climate agreements and EU targets as recorded in the lecture",
        "What to retain"
      ],
      "objective_ids": [
        "O010"
      ],
      "semantic_baseline": "Direct, market, cooperative and information families; banking/borrowing, emission/deposition bubbles, offsets/netting and permit types have distinct boundaries. Smooth interior least-cost fixed-target allocation equalises MAC=λ; taxMAC=t or permitMAC=p. Cost effectiveness does not select optimal target. Tax fixes price and quantity responds; cap fixes covered quantity and price responds; location, coverage, monitoring and enforcement matter. Innovation need not lower every MAC. Historical ETS coverage/price/auction/offset/revenue have distinct units; NA is unavailable, count discrepancy and source dates retained. EU target denominators differ and2040 value is blank; no uncertainty ranking or current-policy verification.",
      "change_class": "new_content",
      "previous_path": null,
      "previous_hash": null,
      "affected_objective_ids": [
        "O010"
      ],
      "inspection_status": "read_finished_note"
    },
    {
      "path": "01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_2/Optimal_Environmental_Taxation.md",
      "sha256": "8da1915b95877f0ba4297c03056ee3110e77fd0d03c58aa602d424cb8c391b1b",
      "bytes": 10512,
      "note_sections": [
        "Optimal Environmental Taxation",
        "Three goals of an environmental tax",
        "Sandmo decomposition and the cost of public funds",
        "Revenue recycling and the double dividend",
        "The final slide's qualifications",
        "What can be applied and what needs further source material"
      ],
      "objective_ids": [
        "O011"
      ],
      "semantic_baseline": "Correction, behaviour and revenue goals differ; excess burden is distinct from transfer, lump-sum tax lacks substitution effect but may have income effect. Simplified Ramsey inverse-elasticity rule is conditional. Sandmo commodity model excludes labour; θ=(1−η)R+ηP, η=1/MCPF, MCPF1 givesP. Expanded N,u_ND,u_D and tax base are undefined, blocking numerical full-model use. Environmental, recycling and interaction effects differ. Weak dividend compares recycling versus lump-sum return of fixed revenue; strong claims an additional overall economic gain and is not guaranteed. Final separability/one-tax qualifications lack full model/proof.",
      "change_class": "new_content",
      "previous_path": null,
      "previous_hash": null,
      "affected_objective_ids": [
        "O011"
      ],
      "inspection_status": "read_finished_note"
    },
    {
      "path": "01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_2/Political_Economy_of_Cost_Benefit_Analysis.md",
      "sha256": "600a646384717bf380b444c4b215804797e786f1224c8bb209fc00d582b00dfe",
      "bytes": 21466,
      "note_sections": [
        "Political Economy of Cost-Benefit Analysis",
        "Topic map",
        "The normative benchmark and its limits",
        "Distinguish use, quality and influence",
        "Four motives for using appraisal",
        "Forecast errors and strategic incentives",
        "Improving the appraisal process",
        "How to interpret a decision that departs from CBA",
        "Sources and page conventions"
      ],
      "objective_ids": [
        "O013"
      ],
      "semantic_baseline": "Decision-informing efficiency benchmark: positive aggregate gain need not be strict Pareto or actual compensation; simplified sum omits discounting and is not a full valuation manual. Use, quality and influence differ. Instrumental, political, communicative and perfunctory motives may coexist. Capacity, bounded rationality and incentives matter. Historical positive-social/negative-financial NPV funding gap creates principal-agent incentives; forecast error alone is not dishonesty. Guidance/expertise need independent scrutiny with effective remit, information and capacity, plus credible fair ex-post accountability. Historical tables do not prove representative/causal/current results. Explaining divergence does not justify it.",
      "change_class": "new_content",
      "previous_path": null,
      "previous_hash": null,
      "affected_objective_ids": [
        "O013"
      ],
      "inspection_status": "read_finished_note"
    },
    {
      "path": "01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_2/Road_Transport_External_Costs.md",
      "sha256": "cc396e42c7c4541affc1793168ef6857bb6cf76f1e847ce27f397ae86f77b0fd",
